# DESCRIPTION

virtio-blk drives a virtio block device (Virtual I/O Device 1.2, section 5.2), as QEMU provides them, and serves it as a disk, sd(3).
devmgr(8) starts one for each device that matches its records (driver(6)), and binds its disks at `/dev/sdF0`, `/dev/sdF1` and so on, 9front's letter for virtio:

```text
driver virtio-blk
class block
match pci 1af4:1042
match pci 1af4:1001
sd F
```

The driver asks the device for its capacity, in 512-byte sectors, whether it is read-only, and whether it keeps a write cache, and for nothing else.
Requests go through one queue, as many at once as it has slots: up to 16, three of the queue's descriptors each, so a queue of 128 entries or more gives 16.
Each slot copies its data through 128 KiB of memory the device reaches through its own DMA; a larger request takes several slots, and one that finds none free waits for one, in order.
The driver refuses a device that does not let the IOMMU translate its addresses (a virtio device must offer `ACCESS_PLATFORM`; QEMU's does with `iommu_platform=on`).
A flush is the device's (`FLUSH`) when it keeps a write cache, and makes durable the writes it has finished; with no cache, writes are durable when done, and a flush is answered at once.
The device's interrupt is MSI-X where devmgr gave the driver an MSI vector, else INTx, and with INTx the driver also polls the queue every 50 ms against a lost interrupt.

# FILES

The driver's tree is served in devmgr(8)'s, at `drivers/virtio-blk-<address>`.

: `sd`
  The disk's sd(3) directory, which devmgr binds at `/dev/sdF`<n>.
: `status`
  Read only.
  `sectors`, `sector` (its size), `read-only` (`true` or `false`), `features` (those in use, in hex) and `interrupt` (`MSI-X` or `INTx`), a line each; then the counts: `requests <n> failures <n>`, a request being a slot's part, and `interrupts <n> polls <n>`.
  If setup failed, `FAILED: status <n>`, with a line before it saying why when the device bypasses the IOMMU.

